WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:06.660
Your learning algorithm will run much better with an appropriate choice of learning rate.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.500
If it's too small, it would run very slowly, and if it's too large, it may not even converge.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.620
Let's take a look at how you can choose a good learning rate for your model.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:23.600
Contrarianly, if you plot the cost for a number of iterations and notice that the cost sometimes

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.540
goes up and sometimes goes down, you should take that as a clear sign that gradient descent

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.660
is not working properly.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.300
This could mean that there's a bug in the code, or sometimes it could mean that your learning

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.340
rate is too large.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.300
So here's an illustration of what might be happening.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:50.220
Here the vertical axis is a cost function j, and the horizontal axis represents a parameter

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:57.780
like maybe w1, and if the learning rate is too big, then if you start off here, your

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.820
update step may overshoot the minimum and end up here.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.220
And in the next update step here, you're gain overshooting, so you end up here, and

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.300
so on.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.940
And that's why the cost can sometimes go up instead of decreasing.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.980
To fix this, you can use a smaller learning rate.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:21.420
So then your updates may start here and go down a little bit and down a bit, and

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.820
will hopefully consistently decrease until it reaches the global minimum.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:32.420
Sometimes you may see that the cost consistently increases after each iteration, like this

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.860
curve here.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.500
This is also likely due to a learning rate that is too large, and it could be addressed

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.940
by choosing a smaller learning rate.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.060
But learning rates like this could also be a sign of a possible bug in the code.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:55.260
For example, if I wrote my code so that w1 gets updated as w1 plus alpha times its

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:01.980
derivative term, this could result in the cost consistently increasing at each iteration.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:07.620
And this is because adding the derivative term moves your cost j further from the global

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.020
minimum instead of closer.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:16.580
So remember, you want to use a minus sign, so the code should be updated, w1 updated

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:22.140
by w1 minus alpha times the derivative term.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:27.620
One debugging tip for a correct implementation of gradient descent is that with a small enough

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:33.700
learning rate, the cost function should decrease on every single iteration.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.580
So if gradient descent isn't working, one thing I will often do, and I hope you find

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:44.540
this tip useful too, one thing I'll often do is just set alpha to be a very, very small

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:51.660
number and see if that causes the cost to decrease on every iteration.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:58.220
If even with alpha set to a very small number, j doesn't decrease on every single iteration,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.500
but instead sometimes increases, then that usually means there's a bug somewhere in

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.740
the code.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.540
Note that setting alpha to be really, really small is meant here as a debugging step.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.420
And a very, very small value of alpha is not going to be the most efficient choice

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.500
for actually training your learning algorithm.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:22.780
One important trade off is that if your learning rate is too small, then gradient descent can

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.020
take a lot of iterations to converge.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:31.460
So when I am running gradient descent, I will usually try a range of values for the learning

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.460
rate alpha.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.620
So I might start by trying a learning rate of 0.001, and I might also try a learning

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:45.260
rate that's 10 times as large, say 0.01 and 0.1 and so on.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:51.100
And for each choice of alpha, you might run gradient descent just for a handful of iterations

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:57.060
and plot the cost function j as a function of the number of iterations.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.380
And after trying a few different values, you might then pick the value of alpha that seems

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.500
to decrease the learning rate rapidly, but also consistently.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.580
In fact, what I actually do is try a range of values like this.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:20.340
After trying 0.001, I'll then increase the learning rate 3-fold to 0.003, and after

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:28.140
that I'll try 0.01, which is again about 3 times as large as 0.003.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.580
So these are roughly trying out gradient descent with each value of alpha being roughly 3 times

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.140
bigger than the previous value.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.860
So what I'll do is try a range of values until I found a value that's too small,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.780
and then also make sure I found a value that's too large, and I'll slowly try to pick the

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:53.700
largest possible learning rate or just something slightly smaller than the largest reasonable

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.820
value that I found.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:00.540
And when I do that, it usually gives me a good learning rate for my model.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.620
So I hope this technique too will be useful for you to choose a good learning rate for

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.820
your implementation of gradient descent.

